{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-9da134d1-d67f-486e-96e1-28c0b5b14b88","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8662f3e094064214","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-y63g4bsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n\nOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context (rhods-operator manager + cloudmanager binaries).\n\n## Triage Outcome\n\nFull decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only). The strategy is S-sized and touches a single repository owned by a single team, but two escape conditions from the below-threshold check apply:\n\n1. **Multiple distinct work streams**: the scope spans code implementation (mechanical conversion of `log.Error` call sites across two binaries plus a CI lint rule) AND substantive content authoring (a new contribution guideline documenting the convention, a DSCI key-migration guide, and publication of the convention for external operator adoption — HLR-8). These are different work-product types with different review cycles, so they are split into separate epics per the work-product boundary rule.\n2. **Genuine unknowns**: the strategy carries open questions (see Health Warnings — Open Questions Triage); none of them changes which epics exist or what they do, so they do not generate Investigation epics, but they were evaluated individually rather than skipped.\n\n## Staff Engineer / SME Input Precedence Log\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n\n| SME correction | Disposition |\n|---|---|\n| \"~16 component action files\" is wrong — exactly 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) | Adopted; scope and HLR-4 use the confirmed 15-file breakdown |\n| Module controller tier (8 module handlers: Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) was missing from the architecture description | Adopted; the module controller tier is explicit in epic scope and HLR-6 |\n| Open Question 5 (\"how many component action files exist\") is based on a wrong premise — all 8 components have exactly one action file (8/8 confirmed by glob) | Adopted; no Investigation epic created for this — the count is confirmed |\n| Three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys, missing `resourceKind`), DSCI key names `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, camelCase convention, cloudmanager shared-reconciler note | Adopted; drives the epic structure and acceptance criteria |\n\nThe Technical Approach section already incorporates these corrections, so no unresolved divergence remains between the two sections.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nThe strategy's single affected component is **rhods-operator** (manager + cloudmanager binaries, owned by the ODH Operator team). `rhods-operator` is not a canonical RHAI Jira component name in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; the closest canonical name is **OpenShift AI Productization** (the RHOAI platform operator is maintained and productized under this component; the downstream repository is `red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator`). All four epics use `OpenShift AI Productization` verbatim. The rhods-operator component exists in architecture context (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`), so it is in-platform — no onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is generated.\n\nThe Cluster Observability Operator team (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is a passive recipient of the published convention; per platform policy overlay 0008, RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies, so no COO-owned epic is generated. Publication of the convention and filing of tracking issues in COO projects are scope items of the documentation epic.\n\n## Step 3 — Investigation Evaluation\n\nAll unknowns were collected from the open questions table, risks, assumptions, and pending reviews, then evaluated against the decision rule (\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\"). See Health Warnings — Open Questions Triage for the full table. Result: **no Investigation epics**. Every open question is either already answered by SME input, a scheduling/content decision that does not change epic structure, or a validation step that is part of an Implementation epic's own work.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager binary reconciliation error log standardization | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log standardization | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | CI lint rule enforcing structured log fields | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Structured log field convention documentation and external publication | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Manager binary log standardization\"]\n    E002[\"E002 Cloudmanager binary log standardization\"]\n    E003[\"E003 CI lint rule\"]\n    E004[\"E004 Convention documentation + publication\"]\n    E001 --> E004\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | The guideline documents the convention as implemented, including before/after examples drawn from the converted manager code and the DSCI key-migration guide; it must describe what was built. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Same — the published convention covers the cloudmanager controllers' log format. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | The contribution guideline references the CI lint rule as the enforcement mechanism for new controller development. |\n| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14/15 | Manager and cloudmanager are separate binaries with no shared code artifacts; the cloudmanager open question (same release vs. subsequent release) is a scheduling decision, not a dependency. The epics are deliberately parallel so cloudmanager can follow in a later release without re-planning. |\n| E003 ∥ E001/E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | The lint rule is CI configuration, not code the conversions build against. The practical ordering concern (an enforcing lint would fail on unconverted call sites) is expressed as an acceptance criterion on the lint epic (ship in warning mode, enforcement documented), never as a DAG edge. |\n\nNo external-dependency edges: the COO team dependency is voluntary adoption outside RHOAI control (platform policy overlay 0008) and does not gate any deliverable in this strategy. The controller-runtime logr dependency is confirmed existing infrastructure.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name/namespace in message strings are converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002, RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | E001 covers the manager binary, E002 the cloudmanager binary (the strategy's component scope is \"manager + cloudmanager binaries\"); E003 provides the CI lint enforcement that is HLR-1's stated measurement mechanism. |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | DSCI is a manager-binary controller; the key migration edits the same `log.Error` call sites as the HLR-1 conversion in `dscinitialization_controller.go`. |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager-binary service controllers. |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module = 15 files) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Action files live in the manager binary and are invoked by its controllers; SME-confirmed exact file list. |\n| HLR-5 | P0 | Cloudmanager controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Separate binary, separate epic so release timing can be decided independently (open question 3). |\n| HLR-6 | P0 | Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Manager-binary module controller tier; SME-corrected scope. |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: HLR-7 edits the same `log.Error` call sites in the same DSC and component controller files as the HLR-1/HLR-3 conversions — adding `resourceKind` is the same mechanical edit on the same surface, so a split would double-touch the same files and duplicate the call-site audit; the HLR is not separately schedulable. |\n| HLR-8 | P1 | Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | docs-authoring epic; priority from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the manager binary standardization epic (RHAISTRAT-8010-E001) is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's own effort estimate (audit ~0.5 sprint + conversion ~0.75–1 sprint) — above the ~2-week guideline. Kept as one logical change: a split by controller tier would duplicate the single call-site audit and fragment one mechanical conversion pass over shared `log.Error` call sites (the DSCI key migration, `resourceKind` additions, and embedded-identity conversion all edit the same call sites). **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic by controller tier (e.g., framework controllers vs. service/module controllers vs. action files) if the audit finds materially more `log.Error` call sites than the strategy's 75%-embedding estimate implies, or if the conversion pass cannot complete within a sprint.\n- **Open Questions Triage** (none gating — no Investigation epics):\n  - *Additional convention fields beyond name/namespace/kind (PM / Observability team)* — does not change which epics exist or what they do: the required field set is fixed by the P0 HLRs, and additional fields are additive follow-up scope. Recorded on the documentation epic as an open content question; the guideline documents the required fields regardless.\n  - *Which existing log-based alerts rely on message-string patterns (SRE / Support)* — the answer does not change epic structure; the inventory and rule updates are part of the implementation epics' backwards-compatibility verification and risk mitigation. SRE/Support turnaround is externally controlled coordination, noted as a risk on the epic, not an AC.\n  - *Cloudmanager same release vs. subsequent release (PM / ODH Operator team)* — a scheduling decision; the epic structure (separate cloudmanager epic, no DAG edge) already supports either answer.\n  - *Actual percentage of log calls embedding identity (ODH Operator team)* — the audit is the first work item of the implementation epics; the conversion happens regardless of the percentage, so the answer changes effort, not structure.\n- **Pending process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, Performance Team Support) are marked \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. These are strategy-level human review gates outside the decomposition's control; epics proceed on the strategy's current content.\n- **Log consumer regression coordination**: identifying and updating text-based alert rules requires SRE/Support input whose timing this strategy does not control. The acceptance criteria sit on the engineering deliverables (before/after log comparison, dev-cluster ingestion verification, documented alert-rule inventory); the cross-team timing is carried as a risk on the manager and cloudmanager epics.\n- **DSCI key-name migration**: existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will break. Mitigation is captured as an AC (single-release transition, release-note documentation with before/after query examples) on the manager epic.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO team adoption of the convention) is voluntary adoption explicitly outside RHOAI control per platform policy overlay 0008; the strategy's deliverable is publishing the convention, which delivers full value regardless of COO timing. No upstream epic gates any downstream work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":44136,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91dd77060b3a4ac5","agent_id":"af4fb60619de8129c","session_id":"852e52ce-bfa6-41ab-90de-21ff2eb47c3f","uuid":"a88eeb67-a627-4fbc-b900-9faef3dd36cc","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:56:16.046Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
