{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-38596f0b-a192-426f-a82d-c64163901f33","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b038458054e82efd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-y63g4bsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E002.md","content":"# Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log standardization\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic standardizes reconciliation error logging across the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary — the companion binary that handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, and CoreWeave. It covers the cloudmanager's reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine), which are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and therefore already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. The epic converts message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs and adds the missing `resourceKind` field, applying the same convention as the manager-binary work so the two binaries emit consistent log fields.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Call-site audit**: identify all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers and any shared reconciler error paths, using grep/static analysis; classify each call site (embeds identity in message string / missing `resourceKind` / non-standard key names if any / already conformant).\n- **Embedded-identity conversion**: for every cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n- **`resourceKind` additions**: add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs (these controllers do not reconcile a single kind implied by a shared request-logger entry point for the downstream resources they manage, so the field is P0 here).\n- **Non-standard key check**: verify whether any cloudmanager error-path log calls use non-standard key names (the strategy marks this \"if applicable\") and migrate any found to the camelCase convention.\n- **Backwards-compatibility verification**: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error; confirm log consumers on the non-OpenShift Kubernetes deployments (e.g., CloudWatch) ingest the new format without configuration changes.\n- **Schedulability**: this epic is deliberately independent of the manager-binary work so that product management can decide whether cloudmanager ships in the same release or a subsequent one (cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n- Given any cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs instead, measured by static analysis flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text across the cloudmanager controllers.\n- Given the audit deliverable, then a checklist of every `log.Error()` call site in cloudmanager reconciliation error paths exists with classification, measured by the checklist covering 100% of call sites found by the agreed grep/static-analysis pass.\n- Given the log format change, then a documented before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error shows structured fields present and confirms the log format remains ingestible by existing log consumers without configuration changes.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-5 (P0)**: cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n- **HLR-1 (P0)**: conversion of message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs — cloudmanager binary portion (the strategy's component scope is rhods-operator manager + cloudmanager binaries).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":50221,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91dd77060b3a4ac5","agent_id":"af4fb60619de8129c","session_id":"852e52ce-bfa6-41ab-90de-21ff2eb47c3f","uuid":"1a297b22-2363-49c7-8246-3a749bbc4e2b","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:57:03.623Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
