{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-4d8f39ec-13cf-4a4f-8e86-94e7c77261ac","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7e2fbb31954f56b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-y63g4bsi/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E003.md","content":"# CI lint rule enforcing structured log fields\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the CI enforcement for the structured log field convention: a lint rule (or static analysis check) that flags `log.Error` calls in rhods-operator reconciliation paths which embed resource name or namespace in the message string or are missing the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`). It is the durable regression-prevention mechanism named in the strategy's acceptance criteria and risk mitigations, and it applies to both the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Lint rule implementation**: a CI-executable check (e.g., a golangci-lint custom rule or a static-analysis script) that detects:\n  - `log.Error` calls in which resource name or namespace values are interpolated into the message string rather than passed as structured key-value pairs;\n  - `log.Error` calls in reconciliation error paths missing required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n- **CI integration**: wire the check into the repository's CI pipeline so it runs on every pull request, covering manager and cloudmanager code.\n- **Phased enforcement**: land the rule in warning/report-only mode initially, because the conversion of existing call sites proceeds in parallel; document the conditions for switching to enforcing mode (once the existing reconciliation error log calls are converted, the rule gates merges).\n- **Local developer workflow**: document how to run the check locally so contributors can self-verify before opening a pull request.\n- **Validation fixtures**: before/after code examples used to validate rule behavior — pre-conversion patterns must be flagged, post-conversion patterns must pass.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a pull request containing a `log.Error` call that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string, when CI runs, then the lint rule flags the call, measured by the rule flagging the pre-conversion validation fixtures in a CI run.\n- Given a pull request containing a conformant `log.Error` call with `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` structured fields, when CI runs, then the lint rule passes it, measured by the rule passing the post-conversion validation fixtures in a CI run.\n- Given the rule runs on the current codebase, then it produces a report of non-conforming call sites consistent with the audit classifications, measured by the rule's output reconciling with the audit checklist for both binaries.\n- Given the enforcement plan, then the rule's warning-mode default and the documented conditions for enabling enforcement are recorded in the repository documentation, measured by the documentation being merged with the rule.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P0, verification mechanism)**: the CI lint rule is the stated measurement mechanism for HLR-1 (\"measured by CI lint rule or static analysis flagging any remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text\") and the risk-mitigation regression guard for the convention across both binaries.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51191,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-91dd77060b3a4ac5","agent_id":"af4fb60619de8129c","session_id":"852e52ce-bfa6-41ab-90de-21ff2eb47c3f","uuid":"6e7ab806-ebd3-46fa-a209-86fedee5716d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:57:18.643Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010"}
