{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88fb055d3eccc49c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E004\n3\ttitle: Structured log field convention documentation and external publication\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n14\tai_signals:\n15\t  change_specificity: 1\n16\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n17\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n18\t  existing_foundation: 1\n19\t  open_questions: 0\n20\t  external_dependency: 0\n21\t  human_process_gates: -1\n22\t  repo_access: 1\n23\t  architecture_claims: 1\n24\tinvestigation_signals: null\n25\tai_implementability: High\n26\tai_implementability_score: 3\n27\tjira_key: null\n28\tbranch: null\n29\tgated_by: null\n30\tgate_failure_impact: null\n31\t---\n32\t# Structured log field convention documentation and external publication\n33\t\n34\t## Description\n35\t\n36\tThis epic authors and publishes the documentation for the structured log field convention: a contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator, plus publication of the convention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (the Cluster Observability Operator team's Loki Operator and Tempo Operator). Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies, so external adoption is best-effort and at the external teams' discretion — this epic's deliverable is the published convention and the filed tracking issues, not external adoption itself.\n37\t\n38\t## Scope\n39\t\n40\tContent outline for the guideline (authored inside this epic):\n41\t\n42\t- **Required fields**: `name`, `namespace` (with the cluster-scoped-resource rule: `namespace` omitted or empty for cluster-scoped kinds), and `resourceKind`; camelCase naming rationale aligning with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions.\n43\t- **Three-problem diagnosis**: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field — the motivation for the convention.\n44\t- **Before/after code examples** for converting message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs, drawn from the implemented conversion across the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n45\t- **DSCI key-migration guide**: before/after query examples for operators with existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the old field names, supporting the single-release transition.\n46\t- **Enforcement reference**: how the CI lint rule guards the convention and how contributors run it locally.\n47\t- **Open content question** (recorded, non-gating): whether the convention should recommend optional fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category) — a PM/Observability-team decision; the guideline documents the required field set regardless, and optional fields would be additive follow-up scope.\n48\t\n49\tPublication scope:\n50\t\n51\t- Publish the convention as the contribution guideline for new controller development in the rhods-operator repository.\n52\t- Publish the convention for external operator adoption and file tracking issues in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects, per the strategy's mitigation for external adoption delay.\n53\t\n54\t## Acceptance Criteria\n55\t\n56\t- Given the convention documentation, when published, then it specifies the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, the cluster-scoped-resource rule, and the three-problem diagnosis, measured by the published guideline containing each element.\n57\t- Given the migration guide, then before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity and before/after query examples for the DSCI key migration are included, measured by the guide containing both example sets reviewed against the implemented code changes.\n58\t- Given the guideline references the implemented behavior, then the content is technically reviewed against the actual conversion work in both binaries, measured by a documented technical review sign-off with no unresolved accuracy findings.\n59\t- Given the external publication, then the convention is published for external operator adoption and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects, measured by links to the published convention and the filed issues.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- **HLR-8 (P1)**: document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9905345d4062dd44","agent_id":"a7474fa0eec5b12ff","session_id":"852e52ce-bfa6-41ab-90de-21ff2eb47c3f","uuid":"0775e94c-85fc-4aaf-9892-c74ecd165ea9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:59:47.168Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88fb055d3eccc49c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
