{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a485a80594c6f963","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log standardization\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tinvestigation_signals: null\n22\tai_implementability: High\n23\tai_implementability_score: 7\n24\tjira_key: null\n25\tbranch: null\n26\tgated_by: null\n27\tgate_failure_impact: null\n28\t---\n29\t# Cloudmanager binary reconciliation error log standardization\n30\t\n31\t## Description\n32\t\n33\tThis epic standardizes reconciliation error logging across the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary — the companion binary that handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, and CoreWeave. It covers the cloudmanager's reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine), which are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and therefore already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. The epic converts message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs and adds the missing `resourceKind` field, applying the same convention as the manager-binary work so the two binaries emit consistent log fields.\n34\t\n35\t## Scope\n36\t\n37\t- **Call-site audit**: identify all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the three cloudmanager controllers and any shared reconciler error paths, using grep/static analysis; classify each call site (embeds identity in message string / missing `resourceKind` / non-standard key names if any / already conformant).\n38\t- **Embedded-identity conversion**: for every cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`).\n39\t- **`resourceKind` additions**: add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all cloudmanager reconciliation error logs (these controllers do not reconcile a single kind implied by a shared request-logger entry point for the downstream resources they manage, so the field is P0 here).\n40\t- **Non-standard key check**: verify whether any cloudmanager error-path log calls use non-standard key names (the strategy marks this \"if applicable\") and migrate any found to the camelCase convention.\n41\t- **Backwards-compatibility verification**: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error; confirm log consumers on the non-OpenShift Kubernetes deployments (e.g., CloudWatch) ingest the new format without configuration changes.\n42\t- **Schedulability**: this epic is deliberately independent of the manager-binary work so that product management can decide whether cloudmanager ships in the same release or a subsequent one (cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes, so its impact scope is narrower).\n43\t\n44\t## Acceptance Criteria\n45\t\n46\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields, measured by unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n47\t- Given any cloudmanager reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the call uses structured key-value pairs instead, measured by static analysis flagging zero remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text across the cloudmanager controllers.\n48\t- Given the audit deliverable, then a checklist of every `log.Error()` call site in cloudmanager reconciliation error paths exists with classification, measured by the checklist covering 100% of call sites found by the agreed grep/static-analysis pass.\n49\t- Given the log format change, then a documented before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error shows structured fields present and confirms the log format remains ingestible by existing log consumers without configuration changes.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- **HLR-5 (P0)**: cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n54\t- **HLR-1 (P0)**: conversion of message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs — cloudmanager binary portion (the strategy's component scope is rhods-operator manager + cloudmanager binaries).\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d438a826920d487","agent_id":"a398ef34c6b494e09","session_id":"852e52ce-bfa6-41ab-90de-21ff2eb47c3f","uuid":"82131a93-9558-4176-ae32-e8748beef28f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T09:07:11.615Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a485a80594c6f963","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010"}
